course: dictionary
lesson: 6013
title: A name with a meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Medicare is a name, so it starts with a capital letter. It is a proper noun, the name of one special thing, like Sydney or Canada. Names can mean different things in different places, so you need context to know which one is meant.
explain: In «Australia's public system, Medicare, is paid by taxes», the word Australia tells you which Medicare is meant. The word public is a clue too: it means run for everyone and paid for by the government.
explain: Ask yourself: which country, which system, what does it pay for? The sentence will usually answer at least one of these questions.
fact: Medicare is a proper noun, so it begins with a capital letter.
fact: In the United States, Medicare is health insurance for people aged 65 and over and some disabled people.
fact: In Australia, Medicare is the public health system that pays for doctors and hospital care for citizens and residents.
fact: In Canada, Medicare is an informal name for public health insurance.
fact: «Covered by Medicare» means the system pays for much or all of the cost, as in «Medicare covers much of the cost of seeing a doctor».
exercise: «Australia's public system, Medicare, is paid by taxes.» Which country's system is this? | United States | Canada | *Australia | England
exercise: Medicare is the name of a health system. What kind of word is it? | An adjective, a describing word | *A proper noun, with a capital letter | A verb, a doing word | A plural noun, more than one thing
exercise: «Medicare covers much of the cost of seeing a doctor.» What does covers mean here? | Draws a picture of | Hides under a blanket | Stops from going | *Pays for part or all of
test: In the United States, who is Medicare for? | *People aged 65 and over and some disabled people | Children under five only | Doctors who work in hospitals | Everyone who lives in Canada
test: What does public mean in «Australia's public system»? | Very old and worn | Only for rich people | Painted in bright colours | *Run by the government for everyone
exam: Why does Medicare start with a capital letter? | At the start of a sentence only | It is a colour | It is a long word | *It is the name of one special health system
